I’d like to talk about a swimming course I took about three years ago. At that time, I went back to my hometown and lived there for about six months. Ever since I was a child, I had wanted to learn how to swim, so I decided to take advantage of the opportunity and finally learn.

I took the course at a local sports center, which had a really warm and welcoming atmosphere. My instructor was a former professional swimmer, and he was excellent at explaining different swimming techniques. We started with some basic skills, such as water safety, breath control, and floating. Then we gradually moved on to more advanced techniques, including leg kicking, arm movements, and eventually breaststroke.

At first, I found it quite challenging because I hadn't been physically active for quite a while, and I wasn't used to being in the water. I also struggled with coordinating my breathing with my movements. But thanks to my instructor's patience and dedication, I gradually became more confident and eventually learned how to swim properly.

Now, I'm confident that I can swim for about one and a half kilometers without getting too tired, which is something I never thought I could do before.

Overall, it was a fantastic experience because I not only learned an important life skill, but I also made some new friends along the way. More importantly, it changed the way I see swimming. I realized that it's not only good for my health, but it's also a great way to relax and unwind after a long day of studying or working. That's why this course left such a strong impression on me.
